e part of a Pure Straight or other patterns. Also, having a concealed hand (if I don't form any melds) could be good for Fully Concealed Hand, which is worth 4 points. However, if I take the Chi, my hand is no longer fully concealed. But since the meld is formed by a discard, it's an exposed meld, so the Fully Concealed Hand is not possible. However, the Chi might help in building a better hand.\\n\\nAlternatively, maybe forming the B3-4-5 Chow allows me to later form another Chow in the same suit. For example, if I have B8, maybe I can get B6 and B7 to form B6-7-8. But B6 has 4 left, B7 has 4. It's possible, but depends on future draws.\\n\\nIn terms of scoring patterns, if I can form multiple Chows in the same suit, that could lead to a Pure Straight (16 points) or even a Pure Shifted Chows (32 points). But I need to see if the current hand can lead there. My T suit has T4, T6-9. If I can form T6-7-8 and T7-8-9, that's two Chows. Plus the B3-4-5, but that's in a different suit. So maybe not. Alternatively, focusing on one suit.\\n\\nAnother thought: my hand has W9 (a terminal tile), which could be part of a All Terminals pattern, but I need more terminals. However, the All Terminals is worth 64 points, but I need only terminals. My current hand has T6-9 (terminals in T suit), but also B3, B4, B5, B8 (non-terminals). So not helpful here.\\n\\nIf I take the Chi B4, I can then have the B3-4-5 Chow, which is a middle Chow. This might be part of a Pure Shifted Chows if I can get consecutive numbers. For example, if I can form B3-4-5 and B6-7-8, that would be two consecutive Chows with a shift of 3. But B6-7-8 would require B6 and B7. Not sure if that's possible.\\n\\nAlternatively, maybe the Chi is not necessary. Since the hand is already 13 tiles, and the melds are empty, perhaps keeping the hand concealed is better for a Fully Concealed Hand (4 points) and possibly other patterns. However, if I don't take the Chi, the previous player's B4 is already played, and if I 